ModMox is going to be a roguelike RPG where you fight your way through a run with a
deck of Magic: the Gathering cards, and the cards you earn along the way can be
changed. A spell might lose a point of its cost, a creature might pick up flying, a
burn spell might stop targeting and hit everything instead. Right now the work is the
part that makes all of that possible: a rules engine for Magic, written in GDScript as
a self-contained Godot addon that knows nothing about the game around it.

Nine milestones in, the engine handles the pregame and London mulligans, every phase
and step of a turn, priority and the stack, lands and mana, casting with targets,
combat with first strike, double strike and trample, state-based actions, triggered
abilities and the layer system for static abilities. The current card pool is
twenty cards from Alpha, from Grizzly Bears and Lightning Bolt to Serra Angel
and Crusade, and every one of them is plain data.

## Why follow the real rules

The obvious way to build a card game is to write each card as a little script. That
falls apart the moment players can modify cards, because a modified card is a card
nobody wrote. So the engine follows the actual structure of the Comprehensive Rules
from the start, priority, the stack, state-based actions, layers with timestamps and
replacement effects, even though the card pool is tiny. Scope gets cut by leaving out
whole mechanics, never by simplifying the architecture, and a rule that only matters
for one mechanic isn't written until a card needs it.

It follows current rules and current Oracle wording, even for Alpha cards. There's no
mana burn and no damage assignment order. If I ever want an old rule back for flavor,
it comes in as a named house rule rather than mixed into the core. Encounters are
always one on one, and there will never be planeswalkers.

## Cards are data

Card Definitions are Godot Resources assembled from a fixed library of primitives:
costs, effects, triggers, static abilities, Values, Filters and Selections. Every
number in an ability is a Value, so "deal 3 damage" can become "deal damage equal to
the number of Goblins you control" without new code. Every effect reaches its
targets through a Selection, so turning "target creature" into "each creature" is a
data change too. If a card can't be built from the primitives, the fix is to add a
primitive, not to script the card.

Rules classifications are worked out from a card's contents instead of being set by
hand. Whether an ability is a mana ability follows CR 605.1a, so a modification that
adds a target to `{T}: Add {R}` correctly turns it into an ability that uses the
stack. A card the player has earned is stored as an untouched base definition plus an
ordered list of Modifications, and the engine flattens it at the start of each
encounter into a printed card that simply is what it is.

## Decisions, replays and undo

The engine never waits for input. Whenever someone has a choice, it stops and returns
a Pending Decision listing the legal options, and it resumes when it gets an answer.
The GUI and the AI answer exactly the same decisions, which keeps everything testable
headless. Priority is answered with an index into the list of legal Actions, so an
illegal play can't even be expressed.

Every encounter is deterministic from its setup and seed, with separate random number
streams for the game and the AI so that tuning the AI never changes a shuffle. That
means a mid-encounter save is just the setup plus the list of answers, and loading
it replays them. Undo works the same way. The engine keeps a checkpoint at each point
where taking something back would be unfair, like a card being drawn, and undoing
restores the checkpoint and replays the few decisions since.

## How it's tested

Each milestone starts by agreeing on its scope and test scenarios, gets built
test-first with GUT, and isn't done until everything is green. The suite is at 321
tests, run headless from one script, with a check for leaked objects. To make sure the
tests actually pin the rules down, I break the rules on purpose at the end of each
milestone and count how many of those mutations the suite catches. Milestone 9 caught
all 24.

## The architecture

- **Engine.** `addons/modmox_rules/`, built from `RefCounted` and `Resource` classes
  with no scene tree dependency, and no game content inside it.
- **Content.** Card Definitions live game-side in `content/cards/` as `.tres` files,
  with a test that checks no two cards share a sub-resource.
- **Events.** Every change to the game goes through one pipeline: proposed, offered
  to replacement effects, applied, logged, then checked for triggers.
- **Layers.** Characteristics are always read through one query, and the layer engine
  works out every object on the battlefield at once, layer by layer, and caches the
  result until the next event.
- **Typing.** Static typing is enforced everywhere, with untyped declarations treated
  as compile errors, and a test fails if that setting is ever undone.
- **Decision records.** Eighteen ADRs cover the engine's design, alongside a glossary
  and a milestone roadmap.

Next up is replacement and prevention effects, and after that the first playable
slice of Alpha against a simple AI. I wrote up how the first nine milestones went in
[A modular Magic engine](/blog/a-modular-magic-engine).
